    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d13">

                <pb facs="0134" n="10" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg10"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00 R 29/499a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Wallace</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-02-19T21:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 19,
                                1921—9 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>102. B–210. For Boyden from Davis.</p>
                <p>Your opinion is desired as to whether it would be advisable to assign
                        Logan,<anchor n="7" xml:id="d13-f1"/> or some other member of the American
                    Unofficial Delegation, to the Embassy at Paris. Such person could keep in touch
                    with the Commission and handle American interests now pending before the
                    Commission and questions which might arise in the future. Your opinion is also
                    desired as to whether Reparation <pb facs="0135" n="11" xml:id="pg11"/>
                    Commission files should be transferred to Embassy or brought to Washington.</p>
                <p>Department desires to be informed regarding date of your sailing and assumes you
                    will come to Washington for conference.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Colby</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d13-f1" n="7" xml:id="d13-f1-content">
                <p>Col. James A. Logan, Jr.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
